  "tips": "## Common Hebrew Idioms\n\nHebrew is full of colorful idioms. Here are some of the most useful:\n\n### ראש גדול (big head) = initiative\n- יש לו ראש גדול -- He takes initiative\n- The opposite is **ראש קטן** (small head) = doing the bare minimum\n- Originally from military slang: a soldier with ראש גדול volunteers for extra tasks\n\n### על הפנים (on the face) = terrible, awful\n- המבחן היה על הפנים -- The test was terrible\n- Used for anything negative or disappointing\n\n### לשבור את הראש (to break the head) = to rack one's brain\n- אני שובר את הראש על זה -- I'm racking my brain over this\n- Used when thinking very hard about a problem\n\n### חבל על הזמן (a pity about the time) = amazing, incredible\n- Literally means \"a waste of time\" but colloquially means \"so good it's beyond words!\"\n- הקפה פה חבל על הזמן! -- The coffee here is amazing!\n\n### לעשות חיים (to make life) = to have fun, party\n- עשינו חיים במסיבה -- We had fun at the party\n- Very common in casual speech\n\n### מה נשמע? (what is heard?) = what's up?\n- The most common casual greeting in Israeli Hebrew\n- מה נשמע, חבר? -- What's up, friend?"
